hi everyone my name is urban fazel  and today i'm going to present you a  recent paper  data driven and non-linear air elastic  models of morphing wings for  control this is joint work with nikola  fonsi and steve brunton and we published  a work in  proceedings of the royal society  a so the goal of this work was to  develop aeroelastic models of  morphing wings and the approach that we  chose is  based on the dynamic  mode decomposition with control and the  idea is to  define models for morphing wings  that are also suitable for control  so the idea of morphing wings  is that we want to  improve the performance of a flight  system by enabling adaptability  over large operating  conditions and the best example for this  can be found in nature  where for example here a falcon  perfectly adapts its wings throughout  this hunting maneuver so it starts  with a high aspect ratio and  gliding and then enters a teardrop  dive to maximize its flight speed  and finally it morphs its wing to this  m-type configuration to generate strong  wing and tail vortices  to improve the maneuverability for a  prey capture  so the bird perfectly adapts its wing  shape throughout  this uh hunting dive by morphing  its wings and our goal is now to also  achieve similar adaptability with our  engineered flight systems  and of course we are not exactly there  yet but there is a lot of research  ongoing in the morphing wings community  so what you can see uh for example in  these two videos is a  recent work from siemens lab at eth  zurich  in the top video you can see a 3d  printed composite  morphing wing and on the lower video  you can see a distributed compliance  lattice  structure morphing wing and the goal of  both of these wings  is first to replace  conventional ailerons by using morphing  for  roll control and then in the case of the  upper wing  we basically placed compliant rips  over the full wingspan  to be able to change the span wise  lift distribution at different flight  conditions  and operating modes and one main  challenge of developing morphing wings  is that they are inherently  flexible so there is a tight coupling  between the structural  and the aerodynamic response and that's  pretty difficult to model and especially  also  to control so the goal is  here to find accurate and efficient  models for morphing wings that are then  also suitable  for control  modeling of morphing wings or flexible  wings in general is not something new  so there's a lot of research or there  was a lot of research over the last  decades and there's still a lot of  research ongoing  so for example what you can see in this  animation  here is a really cool work done by the  group of  professor palacios they  analyze mainly a very flexible wings  and wind turbine blades and what you can  see here  in this animation is a t-tail high  altitude long endurance  vehicle that is encountering a ghost and  they simulate  this very flexible aircraft using their  simulation toolbox  sharpie where they model the wing  structure  as exact beam  elements and couple the structural model  with an unsteady vortex lotis method  and in the case of modeling morphing  wings we use a very similar approach  the big difference in terms of modeling  the structure is that  additionally to the flexibility like the  bending and the torsional flexibility of  the wing  we also want to model the cambering of  the wing so how the  the airfoil actually changes and  therefore instead of using  a beam element beam  or an exact beam element method we use a  more detailed  shell element based finite element model  so what you can see here in this figure  is an exploded view of our high aspect  ratio  morphing wing with the top wing surface  the bottom wing surface and the internal  structure so the internal structure  consisting of the two  spars and the ribs  and in this case we have rigid ribs  at the inner part of the wings from here  to here and on the outer part we have  the morphing ribs so that the compliance  structure that  replace the ailerons  then similar to the unsteady vortex  lattice method  we use a 3d unsteady panel method so we  also reduce the problem to  the surface of the wing and  the wake and ultimately what we solve  for both  methods is a set of  non-linear differential algebraic  equations  and this is very important to consider  if we then later want to  uh develop a reduced order model  but ultimately what is the issue with  this is that  those methods are comparably efficient  but they're still way too slow to be  used for control  so we want to develop a data-driven  reduced order  model of our morphing wing that would  enable us to the model to be used for  control  and specifically we use the method  dynamic mode decomposition with control  dmdc  and we introduce two extensions  extensions to this model the first one  is we introduce a formulation to  a handle the algebraic equation so this  is fundamental as i said before  that's basically the equations that  we solve so we need this extension that  dmdc can also handle these algebraic  equations  and then second we are interested in  developing a reduced order model that is  valid over a large  operating regime and therefore we  introduce an interpolation scheme that  can  connect several admdc models so that we  then finally end up having  a parametric admdc model that is valid  over  a larger flight speed range or operating  range  and uh to start with i want to introduce  what dynamic mode composition actually  does so first  in dmdc we need to collect data  so what you see here for is for example  the flow around the cylinder  we run a numerical simulation and a  different time step  as we take snapshots of our state of  interest in this case of our  flow field and in the case of um  flexible wings of or of our morphing  wing  we are mainly interested in the wing  deformations  and the pressure distribution  on the surface of our wing so we're not  exactly  interested in what's going on in the  flow around the wing but we really want  to know what's happening  on the wing and therefore we define  the state of interest for the morphing  wing  as the wing deformations  and the singularity strength on the wing  surface  and in case of the wing deformations we  don't look at the actual wing  deformations but we look at or  modal modal coordinates already so at  the  structural vibration and morphing  modes as our coordinate system for  the deformations okay and  in terms of the inputs of our model  we basically have two sets of inputs  first uh the wing flight states so  the angle of attack and the rotation  rates  these are like the first four inputs  and then second the morphing  inputs so how we actually deform uh  our wings so when you think about the  videos from the previous  slide and in reality we place for  example servo actuators or some type of  actuators to really  camber so deform our our wing  and those are inputs to our model  and then we run  a simulation so we impulsively  excite our inputs and we take snapshots  at the  different time step and then in a second  step  we organize our data so we have our  snapshots  and we place them or we kind of like  organize them in  three matrices uh x consists  of uh the snapshot at time step one time  step two  etc x prime are the snapshots of our  state  shifted in time so the first column is  the snapshot at the snapshots at time  step 2  then 3 etc and third we have  our input matrix  epsilon that consists of the inputs  at time step one two etc  and then what dm dc does is  it finds or it tries to find a best fit  linear model that advances our state x  into x prime considering the inputs  epsilon and ultimately  so ultimately we want to find the two  linear operators a  and b and how this is done is by  first reformulating this into  x prime so x prime  equals c which is our unknown operators  times sigma which are our known  snapshots  and then by using an svd  we can take the pseudo inverse of sigma  and solve  here for c  and that's basically that's how we can  get our best fit uh  linear model using uh this data  and then in the case of our algebraic  system what we need to do is we need to  consider those algebraic terms  so what we do is that we not just find  the best fit linear model  advancing x into x prime considering the  inputs but additionally we also  consider our algebraic terms so our  time shifted inputs so basically  epsilon primes is similar to x prime is  that we shift our inputs by one time  step so our first column will be  the inputs at time step 2 then 3 etc  but then apart from that we can apply  exactly the same method like  in a dmd c so we reformulate this  formula here so x prime equals  c times uh sigma there it is  we take the pseudo inverse to solve  for c and we have our best fit  linear model okay and then having this  model we can  look at the dynamics so we can  reshape basically our dynamic modes so  we can take the  eigenvalues or approximate approximately  eigenvalues and eigenvectors of our  system  and then reshape the eigenvectors into  let's call it like  eigen wing shapes and eigensingularity  strength  with the corresponding time dynamics  we can then that's basically what we  want to have  uh predict future states so we can  advance  our state x into uh x at time step and  into  the state x at time time step n plus one  considering our inputs and our algebraic  terms  and then the last thing very important  is that those states  might be comparably high dimensional so  this model might still be very slow and  not be applicable for control  so actually what we do is that we never  really  need to calculate those large matrices  a b and f or  combined the matrix c but we directly  project it onto the dominant modes svd  modes  of our output space x prime  and this gives us then a super compact  uh  reduced order uh model of our dynamics  so basically we can  directly without ever calculating see  get our  dynamic matrices or the operators a b  and f tilde and what is really nice  about this method is that we can still  go  between those reduced states to  the full physical states by using our  dominant svd modes of the output spacex  prime okay so this is how we  define and build our dmdc or admdc  model then in our paper we show or we  apply  this to three examples first the generic  and differential algebraic system of  equations  where we show that it's not only  important to use  this algebraic extension to get accurate  results but if we don't use this  extension we get  unstable system dynamics  second we apply admdc to  aerodynamics only problem so to a naka  double o 12  rigid wing and then finally we apply the  method  to our morphing wing so as i mentioned  before the state of interest  is the wing deformations and the  singularity strength  and the inputs to our model are the  flight state so  angle of attack three rotation rates and  um the morphing actuation  and then in the first step we train our  model so that's basically  the two steps that i mentioned before so  collecting data  and organizing data so we  um impulsively excite  our inputs in a random order and take  snapshots  of our state then we can build our admdc  model  and we then have  two test cases so first we test  our model by using  sinusoidal inputs with different reduced  frequencies in the direction of the  different inputs  and second we use  a different like a random set of  impulsive  inputs in the direction yeah in the  direction of our inputs  yeah and with those testing phases what  we basically want to do is we want to  compare  our full and reduced order model so  what you see here in this plot is first  the  lift coefficient and the drag  coefficient and the roll coefficient  the yellow continuous line is the  reduced order model  and the blue dash line is the full model  and  what we get is like really excellent  agreement between  the full and the reduced order model and  on top of that we get  a speed up of three orders of magnitude  so our reduced order model is super  accurate  and also super fast okay  so um so far i showed you results for a  single  reduced order model so a model that is  valid at a specific  operating condition but as i mentioned  before we're actually interested in  developing a reduced order model that is  valid over a larger  range of operating conditions so what we  then  basically do is that because  we have those super fast  compact models our idea is to simply  run several local reduced order models  in parallel  project the local reduced state  up to our full physical state and then  interpolate the physical state  so what we do is we have our inputs  then in this schematic we have three  local models  so one two three and then  each for each local model  we advance our reduced state q  a time step n to q a time step n  plus one by considering the inputs and  the algebraic terms  and then project the reduced state up  to our full physical state and then  interpolate  the full physical state at our operating  condition of interest so in this case at  the flight speed  of interest and that's basically that's  how we  interpolate several local reduced order  models  we then also test this approach  using the similar set of inputs so first  different sinusoidal inputs  with different reduced frequencies and  then the impulsive inputs in a second  testing phase but compared  to the plot that i showed before  additionally here  we sweep over a velocity range so  basically we accelerate our wing  from 35 to 80 meters per second  and again we get excellent agreement  between the reduced and the full  model and even introducing this  interpolation  we still get a speed up of three orders  of magnitude  so then last to show  the performance of our modeling approach  we incorporate the admdc model into a  model predictive control framework and  we run  several control examples so for example  we try to mitigate  gusts or we try to follow a specific  lift coefficient and trajectory  and then ultimately we want to use our  admdc model  to control the loads on an airborne wind  energy drone  and what an airborne wind energy drone  is it's basically a  tethered drone that is used to harvest  wind energy and the idea of using  this admdc a model predictive control is  to be able to fly closer to the maximum  wing loading and closer to the maximum  tether loading so that we can basically  improve uh the performance of this uh  airborne  uh wind energy system and in our paper  we show that we can indeed uh reduce uh  gust peaks of such an airborne uh wind  energy  drone okay  so to summarize we developed a data  driven  reduced order model of a morphing wing  we showed its  performance in terms of accuracy and  speed up  and we also applied or used the admdc  model for model predictive control  and you can find the link to the paper  in the description of the video below  you can also find a link to nikola skit  top  for the code and additionally  i added some links to the work of  professor palacios  and to the work that we recently  recently published at sema's lab  in terms of morphing wings thanks for  watching

